+## TL;DR
+- `Meilisearch`, `Typesense`, and `Qdrant` are strong starting points for AI-powered catalog search and semantic product discovery.
+- `RecBole`, `Microsoft Recommenders`, and `NVIDIA Merlin` are the best fit when you need recommendation pipelines instead of just search.
+- `Rasa`, `Flowise`, `Langflow`, and `AnythingLLM` are practical picks for self-hosted support agents and guided buying assistants.
+- `PaddleOCR`, `Docling`, and `Unstructured` are useful for turning supplier PDFs, invoices, and catalogs into structured ecommerce data.
+- `InvokeAI`, `Fooocus`, `rembg`, and `Real-ESRGAN` cover the most common product-image workflows: generation, cleanup, background removal, and upscaling.
+
+## Why This List
+Open source AI tools matter in ecommerce because catalog quality, search relevance, merchandising logic, and customer support flows are rarely one-size-fits-all. The best AI tools for ecommerce 2026 are the ones teams can inspect, run in their own stack, and connect to storefronts, PIMs, ERPs, and data warehouses without surrendering roadmap control.
+
+This list is intentionally strict. Every entry links to a public GitHub repository, excludes SaaS-only products, and favors tools that support practical ecommerce outcomes such as product search, recommendations, support automation, catalog enrichment, and media operations. Where the ecosystem is thin, that gap is called out instead of being padded with weak matches.
